WEBVTT


00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
Downloaded @ subs4free.club

﻿1

00:01:47.982 --> 00:01:49.442
<i>It does now seem like</i>


00:01:49.526 --> 00:01:52.237
that sometime
in the last couple of days


00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.447
there's been perhaps
the worst accident


00:01:54.531 --> 00:01:55.573
in the short history


00:01:55.657 --> 00:01:57.492
of the world's nuclear
power industry.


00:01:57.575 --> 00:01:59.887
<i>In Moscow,
Western diplomats have spent the day</i>


00:01:59.911 --> 00:02:01.538
<i>trying to get more information</i>


00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:04.207
<i>about the accident
with no success.</i>


00:02:25.436 --> 00:02:28.565
<i>A zone with a
19-mile radius around Chernobyl</i>


00:02:28.648 --> 00:02:31.276
<i>remains a poisoned
no man's land.</i>


00:02:58.887 --> 00:03:01.723
Soviet television tonight
accused the western media


00:03:01.806 --> 00:03:03.766
of spreading
slanderous inventions


00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:05.351
about the Chernobyl accident.


00:34:30.568 --> 00:34:33.029
<i>It's now
clear that the Soviet Union</i>


00:34:33.112 --> 00:34:35.281
<i>has suffered one of
the worst disasters</i>


00:34:35.364 --> 00:34:37.450
<i>in the history of nuclear power.</i>


00:34:37.533 --> 00:34:39.452
Massive quantities of radiation


00:34:39.535 --> 00:34:41.704
have apparently been
released in an accident


00:34:41.788 --> 00:34:44.540
at the Chernobyl Power
Station in the Ukraine.


00:34:44.624 --> 00:34:46.375
The Soviet version is this,


00:34:46.459 --> 00:34:48.211
one of the atomic reactors


00:34:48.294 --> 00:34:50.004
at the Chernobyl
Atomic Power Plant


00:34:50.087 --> 00:34:51.881
in the city of Kyiv was damaged


00:34:51.964 --> 00:34:53.966
and there is
speculation in Moscow


00:34:54.050 --> 00:34:56.469
that people were injured
and may have died.


00:34:56.552 --> 00:34:58.346
The Soviets may
have been fairly quick


00:34:58.429 --> 00:34:59.847
to acknowledge the accident


00:34:59.931 --> 00:35:03.518
because evidence in the form
of mild and nuclear radiation


00:35:03.601 --> 00:35:06.187
have already reached
beyond the Soviet borders


00:35:06.270 --> 00:35:07.146
to Scandinavia.


00:35:07.230 --> 00:35:08.457
<i>There are indications</i>


00:35:08.481 --> 00:35:09.857
<i>the accident could have happened</i>


00:35:09.941 --> 00:35:10.733
<i>several days ago.</i>


00:35:10.817 --> 00:35:11.818
<i>It would take that long</i>


00:35:11.901 --> 00:35:14.111
<i>for radiation to
drift to Scandinavia</i>


00:35:14.195 --> 00:35:15.863
<i>and to make the
political decision</i>


00:35:15.947 --> 00:35:16.823
<i>to announce it.</i>


00:35:16.906 --> 00:35:17.740
You can be sure


00:35:17.824 --> 00:35:19.951
that the decision
to, to disclose


00:35:20.034 --> 00:35:22.411
was made at the
very highest level,


00:35:22.495 --> 00:35:24.580
undoubtedly by
Gorbachev himself.


00:35:24.664 --> 00:35:26.058
<i>The Soviet press is paying</i>


00:35:26.082 --> 00:35:28.125
<i>signally little attention
to the disaster.</i>


00:35:28.209 --> 00:35:29.544
<i>Newspapers carry only</i>


00:35:29.627 --> 00:35:31.128
<i>the brief official announcement</i>


00:35:31.212 --> 00:35:32.839
<i>inconspicuously placed.</i>


00:35:32.922 --> 00:35:34.442
<i>People have noticed
the small paragraph</i>


00:35:34.507 --> 00:35:36.717
<i>but seemed unaware
of the potential dangers.</i>


00:35:36.801 --> 00:35:39.262
<i>People in
the cities, towns, and villages</i>


00:35:39.345 --> 00:35:40.221
<i>of the Ukraine</i>


00:35:40.304 --> 00:35:41.889
<i>just haven't been told</i>


00:35:41.973 --> 00:35:44.350
<i>what dangers they may face.</i>


00:41:25.274 --> 00:41:28.277
On the eve of the Seven
Nation Summit in Tokyo


00:41:28.360 --> 00:41:30.237
both Mrs. Thatcher
and President Reagan


00:41:30.321 --> 00:41:33.199
have urged the Russians to
say more about Chernobyl.


00:41:33.282 --> 00:41:35.409
<i>Meeting
with Italy's Premier Craxi,</i>


00:41:35.492 --> 00:41:38.579
<i>Mr. Reagan was demanding
answers from the Russians.</i>


00:41:38.662 --> 00:41:42.166
Come forward and tell
everybody exactly what happened.


00:41:42.249 --> 00:41:47.046
You can hardly call this kind
of an accident an internal affair


00:41:47.129 --> 00:41:49.632
when other countries
are threatened


00:41:49.715 --> 00:41:52.259
with the fallout of ra...


00:41:52.343 --> 00:41:54.220
dangerous radioactivity.


00:41:54.303 --> 00:41:55.343
A report in this morning's


00:41:55.387 --> 00:41:57.431
Communist party
newspaper "Pravda"


00:41:57.514 --> 00:41:59.600
indicates that the fire
at the power station


00:41:59.683 --> 00:42:01.310
still may not have been put out


00:42:01.393 --> 00:42:03.646
ten days after it started.


00:42:03.729 --> 00:42:05.606
The newspaper says
there was an explosion


00:42:05.689 --> 00:42:08.150
followed by two
fires at the plant.


00:42:08.234 --> 00:42:10.462
<i>The "Pravda" pays
tribute to the firemen whose efforts</i>


00:42:10.486 --> 00:42:13.239
<i>it says limited the scale of
the damage considerably.</i>


00:42:13.322 --> 00:42:15.491
<i>"Brave bold men" it calls them,</i>


00:42:15.574 --> 00:42:16.700
<i>fighting courageously,</i>


00:42:16.784 --> 00:42:19.078
<i>hardly able to breathe
for soot and smoke,</i>


00:42:19.161 --> 00:42:20.996
<i>their boots sticking
in the asphalt</i>


00:42:21.080 --> 00:42:22.915
<i>melted by the high temperatures.</i>


00:46:04.052 --> 00:46:07.514
<i>As an
American I'm very privileged</i>


00:46:07.598 --> 00:46:09.850
<i>to be able to represent</i>


00:46:09.933 --> 00:46:13.687
<i>in the Soviet Union the
concern of Americans.</i>


00:46:22.404 --> 00:46:24.072
We have agreed


00:46:24.156 --> 00:46:28.785
to a joint Soviet-American
effort in this regard.


00:53:44.054 --> 00:53:46.973
<i>Until the accident,
there were 50 nuclear reactors</i>


00:53:47.057 --> 00:53:48.683
<i>operating in the Soviet Union</i>


00:53:48.767 --> 00:53:51.603
<i>generating 11% of
that nation's electricity.</i>


00:53:51.686 --> 00:53:53.897
<i>Most of the larger Soviet plants</i>


00:53:53.980 --> 00:53:56.232
<i>differ in design
from US reactors.</i>


00:53:56.316 --> 00:53:57.817
<i>The major difference is</i>


00:53:57.901 --> 00:54:00.403
<i>most Soviet nuclear
plants do not have</i>


00:54:00.487 --> 00:54:01.821
<i>containment buildings,</i>


00:54:01.905 --> 00:54:03.615
<i>the thick concrete
dome structures</i>


00:54:03.698 --> 00:54:07.327
<i>Americans are used to
seeing built around US reactors.</i>


00:54:07.410 --> 00:54:10.038
<i>When the worst American
nuclear accident occurred</i>


00:54:10.121 --> 00:54:11.164
<i>at Three Mile Island,</i>


00:54:11.247 --> 00:54:13.124
<i>most leaking radiation
was kept inside</i>


00:54:13.208 --> 00:54:14.542
<i>the containment building.</i>


00:54:14.626 --> 00:54:18.213
<i>If as seems to be the case
there was no containment</i>


00:54:18.296 --> 00:54:20.423
around the reactor
as is the general...


00:54:20.507 --> 00:54:21.817
Can we be
sure of that? What do you...


00:54:21.841 --> 00:54:23.176
I do not, I do not know.


00:54:23.259 --> 00:54:24.820
Do you think
there's a containment building?


00:54:24.844 --> 00:54:27.013
I don't think that there
is a containment building


00:54:27.097 --> 00:54:32.769
because the policy of
Soviet atomic constructors


00:54:32.852 --> 00:54:35.146
was that the reactor's
absolutely safe


00:54:35.230 --> 00:54:38.483
and so there is no need to
have this kind of precaution


00:54:38.566 --> 00:54:41.361
because it indicate
possibility of disaster


00:54:41.444 --> 00:54:43.989
which Russian
always con-considered,


00:54:44.072 --> 00:54:46.491
uh, absolutely impossible.


00:54:46.574 --> 00:54:48.302
So going to be clear about
this, you're, you're going


00:54:48.326 --> 00:54:51.830
as far as to say you think it
almost certain or just probable


00:54:51.913 --> 00:54:55.583
there was no protective
shield around this reactor


00:54:55.667 --> 00:54:57.210
which appears to
have melted down?


00:54:57.293 --> 00:54:59.421
No, I think that
there is no protection,


00:54:59.504 --> 00:55:01.506
protection shield
around this reactor's


00:55:01.589 --> 00:55:03.008
certainly the case,


00:55:03.091 --> 00:55:05.218
because the building
as such indicate


00:55:05.301 --> 00:55:06.177
that there is no shield.


00:55:06.261 --> 00:55:08.179
Walter Patterson, if that is so,


00:55:08.263 --> 00:55:09.639
what is the danger to life?


00:55:09.723 --> 00:55:13.059
Well, obviously if radioactivity
got as far as Scandinavia,


00:55:13.143 --> 00:55:15.562
how long, however
long it took to get there,


00:55:15.645 --> 00:55:18.815
a great deal must have
escaped close to the site.


00:56:56.246 --> 00:56:59.415
What has been remarkable
is how much the Soviet people


00:56:59.499 --> 00:57:02.669
in spite of all the evidence
still seem to be believing


00:57:02.752 --> 00:57:05.672
the fairy stories the
government is still telling them.


00:57:08.258 --> 00:57:09.509
It could be argued, Martin,


00:57:09.592 --> 00:57:12.387
that only the Soviet
Union could have kept a lid


00:57:12.470 --> 00:57:15.265
on what might have been
a situation of public panic,


00:57:15.348 --> 00:57:19.018
only a totalitarian state
could have got away with it.


00:57:19.102 --> 00:57:20.812
I'm not sure they
have got away with it.


01:32:03.851 --> 01:32:05.495
<i>Sunday's outbreak of fighting</i>


01:32:05.519 --> 01:32:07.897
<i>is only the latest in
four months of violence</i>


01:32:07.980 --> 01:32:10.149
<i>in the tiny Caucasian
republic of Georgia.</i>


01:32:10.233 --> 01:32:12.693
<i>Spasms of
unrest in the Soviet Union</i>


01:32:12.777 --> 01:32:14.362
<i>were felt in the
southern republics</i>


01:32:14.445 --> 01:32:16.739
<i>of Azerbaijan and Armenia.</i>


01:32:33.506 --> 01:32:36.133
<i>The rebellion
against Communist authority</i>


01:32:36.217 --> 01:32:37.593
<i>has now spread</i>


01:32:37.677 --> 01:32:40.096
<i>and there's almost certainly
more trouble to come.</i>


01:32:40.179 --> 01:32:41.782
<i>The decision to send in troops</i>


01:32:41.806 --> 01:32:43.683
<i>was taken by
President Gorbachev.</i>

